        "description": "<div id=\"remove_no_follow\">\n\t\t<div class=\"grid grid--cols-10@md grid--cols-8@lg article-column \">\n\t\t\t\t\t  <div class=\"col-12 col-10@md col-6@lg col-start-3@lg\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"article-column__content\">\n<section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For most of the history of enterprise software, accountability has been relatively easy to understand. A person logs in, a person has a set of permissions, and a person takes an action. If something goes wrong, you can usually work backwards from there.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Agentic AI changes that. We are moving from systems that suggest what someone should do to systems that can actually do it. Agents can access tools, call other systems, make decisions across multiple steps, and increasingly operate without a human approving every move. That is the point, of course. An autonomous system that needs someone hovering over every decision is not particula",
