        "description": "<div id=\"remove_no_follow\">\n\t\t<div class=\"grid grid--cols-10@md grid--cols-8@lg article-column \">\n\t\t\t\t\t  <div class=\"col-12 col-10@md col-6@lg col-start-3@lg\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"article-column__content\">\n<section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Most teams building AI agents are pouring effort into better memory, longer context windows, conversation summaries, retrieval systems that pull up anything the agent has ever seen. These are useful tools. But they do not solve the problem of knowing whether an action actually completed. Memory tells an agent what happened. It doesn\u2019t tell the agent what\u2019s true right now. And for any agent that takes real action \u2014 refunding money, changing an order, updating an account \u2014 that distinction is the whole ballgame.</p>\n\n\n<div class=\"extendedBlock-wrapper block-coreImage undefined\"><figure data-wp-context='{\"imageId\":\"6ad09827e6992\"}' d",
